ois district that leans slightly Democratic. Every major race rater places the contest in the Safe or Solid Democratic column, and prediction markets give Sorensen roughly a 96 to 98 percent chance of winning. No public general-election polls are available, so the outlook rests on fundamentals and expert assessments.\n\nSorensen has built a large financial advantage, raising about $2.2 million and holding $1.3 million on hand, while Republican nominee Dillan Vancil has raised about $309,000 with $57,000 available. Sorensen has emphasized bipartisan work on water funding and local projects, and local editorial coverage has criticized Vancil's candidacy. The district's modest Democratic lean, the incumbent's resources, and a favorable national environment point to a comfortable Sorensen win, though the lack of polling leaves some room for uncertainty.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9829, "margin": 14.81}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9843, "margin": 15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9781, "margin": 15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9731, "margin": 14.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9707, "margin": 15.0}]}